.SH NAME
ktm-guide-first-program \- A first program (chapter 1 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
What a ktm source file looks like, where a program starts, how it prints, how it ends, and the ways to build and run it.
.SH Hello
.PP
A program starts at \fBmain\fR\&. \fBprintf\fR prints, as in C.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    printf("Hello\en");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
Hello
.EE
.RE
.PP
Save it as \fBhello.ktm\fR\&. There are three ways to run it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ktm hello.ktm \-o hello && ./hello    # compile to a native binary, then run it
ktm \-run hello.ktm                   # compile to a temporary binary and run it
ktm \-jit hello.ktm                   # compile in memory and run, no file written
.EE
.RE
.PP
The binary needs nothing installed to run: ktm's library is linked into it. \fB\-jit\fR is the fastest way to try something; a binary is what you deploy.
.SH Three forms of main
.PP
\fBmain\fR returns nothing, or the program's exit code, and can take the command line.
.PP
.TS
allbox tab(	);
lb lb
l lx .
T{
Form
T}	T{
Exit code
T}
T{
\fBvoid main()\fR
T}	T{
0
T}
T{
\fBi32 main()\fR
T}	T{
what it returns
T}
T{
\fBi32 main(i32 argc, u8 **argv)\fR
T}	T{
what it returns; \fBargv\fR holds the arguments
T}
.TE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
    printf("returning 3\en");
    return 3;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
returning 3
.EE
.RE
.PP
The shell sees 3 as the exit status (\fBecho $?\fR). With arguments:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main(i32 argc, u8 **argv) {
    printf("%d argument(s)\en", argc);
    printf("the program is called %s\en", argc > 0 ? "something" : "nothing");
    for (i64 i = 1; i < argc; i++) {
        printf("argument %d: %s\en", i, argv[i]);
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 argument(s)
the program is called something
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBargv[0]\fR is the program's own name, so a program run with no arguments has \fBargc == 1\fR\&. After \fBktm \-jit file.ktm\fR or \fBktm \-run file.ktm\fR, everything after the file name goes to the program: \fBktm \-jit tool.ktm \-\-verbose input.txt\fR\&.
.SH What goes where
.PP
A file holds declarations: imports, types, functions, global variables and constants. Statements go inside functions. Imports come first.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
const i64 LIMIT = 3;
i64 gCalls = 0;

struct Point { i64 x; i64 y; };

i64 manhattan(Point p) {
    gCalls++;
    return p.x + p.y;
}

void main() {
    Point p = {2, 5};
    for (i64 i = 0; i < LIMIT; i++) {
        manhattan(p);
    }
    printf("%d after %d calls\en", manhattan(p), gCalls);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
7 after 4 calls
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
A function is declared before it is called: define it above its first call, or declare it there with a prototype (see Functions (see ktm-guide-functions(7))).
.IP \(bu 2
Globals, types and constants are visible from their declaration to the end of the file.
.IP \(bu 2
A global's initial value can run code. Those run in order, before \fBmain\fR\&.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i64 square(i64 n) { return n * n; }

i64 gBase = 7;
i64 gArea = square(gBase);    // runs before main

void main() {
    printf("%d\en", gArea);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
49
.EE
.RE
.SH Comments
.PP
Line comments start with \fB//\fR, block comments are \fB/* ... */\fR, as in C. Block comments don't nest.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
/* A block comment
   over several lines. */
void main() {
    // to the end of the line
    printf("comments are ignored\en");   /* or in the middle */
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
comments are ignored
.EE
.RE
.SH Printing
.PP
\fBprintf\fR takes a format and the values for it, one value for each conversion (\fB%d\fR, \fB%s\fR\&...).
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    printf("plain text\en");
    printf("%s is %d years old\en", "Ada", 36);
    printf("%x %X %o\en", 255, 255, 8);
    printf("%c%c\en", 'o', 'k');
    printf("%f %.2f %e\en", 3.14159, 3.14159, 31415.9);
    printf("100%%\en");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
plain text
Ada is 36 years old
ff FF 10
ok
3.141590 3.14 3.141590e+04
100%
.EE
.RE
.PP
Widths and flags work as in C: a number is the minimum width, \fB\-\fR aligns left, \fB0\fR pads with zeros, \fB\&.n\fR is the precision.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    printf("[%5d] [%\-5d] [%05d]\en", 42, 42, 42);
    printf("[%8.3f] [%\-8.1f]\en", 2.5, 2.5);
    printf("[%6s] [%\-6s] [%.2s]\en", "ab", "ab", "abcdef");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
[   42] [42   ] [00042]
[   2.500] [2.5     ]
[    ab] [ab    ] [ab]
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fB%d\fR, \fB%u\fR, \fB%x\fR and \fB%o\fR print all 64 bits. \fB%ld\fR works too, but isn't needed.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB%s\fR takes a C string (\fBu8 *\fR), a slice (\fBu8[]\fR) or a \fBString\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
The first argument is always a format: a literal \fB%\fR is written \fB%%\fR\&.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

void main() {
    i64 big = 5000000000;
    printf("%d %ld\en", big, big);

    u8[] word = "slice";
    String owned = str.from("String");
    printf("%s, %s and %s\en", "C string", word, owned);
    owned.free();
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
5000000000 5000000000
C string, slice and String
.EE
.RE
.SS ktm's own conversions
.PP
\fB%Q\fR prints a string escaped the way it would be written in source code. \fB%C\fR prints a character in upper case.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    printf("%Q\en", "say \e"hi\e"\en");
    printf("%C%c\en", 'h', 'i');
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
say \e"hi\e"\en
Hi
.EE
.RE
.SS Checking the values
.PP
The compiler checks a literal format against its values: one value for each conversion, of a type the conversion prints. A mistake is an error at compile time instead of garbage, or a crash, at run time.
.PP
A missing value:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    printf("%d and %d\en", 1);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
printf format expects 2 arguments but 1 was supplied
.EE
.RE
.PP
One too many:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    printf("%d\en", 1, 2);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
printf format expects 1 argument but 2 were supplied
.EE
.RE
.PP
A number where text goes:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    printf("%s\en", 42);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
`%s` in a `printf` format prints text (a u8 *, a u8[] or a String), not `i64`
.EE
.RE
.PP
An integer for a float conversion, which would print garbage:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 total = 7;
    printf("%.2f\en", total);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
`%f` in a `printf` format prints a float (an integer goes in as one with (f64)n), not `i64`
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same checks apply to \fBstr.format\fR, \fBos.eprintf\fR, \fBlog.info\fR and the other functions that take a format, and to \fBthrow\fR\&'s message.
.SH Printing to stderr
.PP
\fBos.eprintf\fR is \fBprintf\fR to stderr, checked the same way.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import os;

void main() {
    printf("to stdout\en");
    os.eprintf("warning: %s\en", "disk almost full");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
to stdout
warning: disk almost full
.EE
.RE
.SH Ending the program
.PP
Returning from \fBmain\fR ends the program with its exit code. \fBos.exit(code)\fR ends it from anywhere.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import os;

void check(i64 n) {
    if (n < 0) {
        printf("negative: %d\en", n);
        os.exit(2);
    }
    printf("ok: %d\en", n);
}

void main() {
    check(5);
    check(\-1);
    printf("never printed\en");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
ok: 5
negative: \-1
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBos.exit\fR ends the program at once: the \fBdefer\fR statements of the functions still running don't run. Returning from \fBmain\fR runs them. See defer and scoped (see ktm-guide-defer-and-scoped(7)).
.SH Scripts
.PP
ktm skips a first line that starts with \fB#!\fR, so a source file can run as a script:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#!/usr/bin/env \-S ktm \-jit
void main() { printf("Hello from a script\en"); }
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
chmod +x hello.ktm
\&./hello.ktm
.EE
.RE
.SH What's next
.PP
Types and values (see ktm-guide-types-and-values(7)) covers the built-in types and how to write numbers, characters and text.
